// THE COLOCATION SELECTION (operator direction 2026-08-18: price and shop colocation near
// Jersey City, extdeps-model every local operator, and run the selection as a Pareto
// standing). The cited facility and plan rows live in the public repo's extdeps.colo
// modules; THIS module owns everything that is ours rather than the world's - which
// facilities are candidates, the distance estimates, the business refusals, the reference
// loads, and the derived request-for-quote list. A quote-only facility enters the selection
// and lands NotComputable with its RFQ named, which makes the shopping to-do
// list a derived projection of the model rather than a note beside it.

// Distances are PLANNING ESTIMATES of road distance from Jersey City, derived from the
// cited street addresses by map reading on 2026-08-18. They are deliberately coarse - the
// axis exists to separate a two-mile facility from a ninety-five-mile one, not to rank
// six against eight.
data natcoweb_published_but_refused_note: NonEmptyStr = "The Natcoweb plans are the census's only other published prices, and every one of them lands NotComputable rather than on the comparable field: their power is amps with no stated voltage, so the power reading is a typed absence and the spine refuses the comparison. That is the correct standing derived rather than asserted - the sticker prices bound space cost but cannot be compared watt-for-watt against the InterServer tiers until a quote supplies voltage and usable continuous watts (and bandwidth-upgrade pricing for the 10 Mbps commit)."

data distance_basis_note: NonEmptyStr = "Road-distance planning estimates from Jersey City, read off the cited addresses 2026-08-18; not measured drives. The operator-burden directive prices travel, so this axis gates before any quote does at the Pennsylvania end of the census."

type OfferPricing
  = PublishedPlan { plan: RetailPlanRow }
  | QuoteOnly { rfq: NonEmptyStr }

// DISTANCE IS EVIDENCE, AND WE DO NOT HAVE IT. The old field was a bare Int named "estimate", and
// this module said in its own prose that the number was deliberately coarse and not meant to rank
// nearby facilities. Feeding it to a selection axis as a point contradicted that: [7,7] asserts the
// road distance IS seven miles, so a seven-mile facility beat an eight-mile one on the strength of
// two guesses. The estimate is retained because it is genuinely useful for eyeballing a shortlist,
// but it is now carried in an arm that CANNOT reach the frontier, and the only arm that can is the
// one holding a measured route. Nothing here invents a width: there is no retained bound, receipt or
// rounding protocol to derive one from, so the honest state is absence, not a guessed corridor.
type DistanceEvidence
  = DistanceMeasured { interval_miles: OrderedClosedInterval<Int>, receipt: NonEmptyStr }
  | DistanceUnmeasured { planning_estimate_miles: Int, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

type ColoOfferCandidate {
  id: NonEmptyStr
  facility: ColoFacilityRow
  pricing: OfferPricing
  distance: DistanceEvidence
}

